Expected Results: Under secure conditions, vulnerability submissions and their attachments should be processed in an isolated sandbox. Any administrative triage components (such as automated parsing engines, AI/LLM review pipelines, or human reviewer interfaces) must not leak internal credentials, cloud storage signatures (e.g., GoogleAccessId, service account details), or backend exceptions. Reviewers and automated systems must be subject to rigorous cryptographic logging to prevent unauthorized 'shadow fixes' or raw exploit leakage.

Actual Results: The automated triage parser executes untrusted inputs using internal service accounts (scrape-bucket-accessor@firecrawl.iam.gserviceaccount.com), leaking signed Google Cloud Storage URLs, HMAC signatures, and raw LLM backend exceptions (litellm.BadRequestError / AnthropicException). This breaks the confidentiality boundary of the platform and permits unauthorized metadata ingestion, exposing active reports to interception and silent remediation without appropriate reward tracking.

Possible Fixes: Implement a strict 'human-in-the-loop' verification system for all automated parsing components. Enforce absolute input sanitization on all uploaded report assets before passing them to LLM or OCR parsers. Ensure all backend exceptions are caught globally and returned as generic error IDs instead of raw stack traces or signed GCP URLs. Enforce dual-authorization controls on all administrative actions to ensure reports cannot be triaged or resolved without explicit multi-party cryptographic sign-off.

Injection Vector / Chain of Custody (How it got put there): The malicious vector is introduced by embedding adversarial metadata inside images or documents uploaded during the report submission process. The automated triage pipeline ingests this file, passes it to an external parser (e.g., firecrawl/litellm), and fails to sanitize the resulting API errors, rendering them directly to the client.

